※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ A function follows the language's own calling convention -- `pl4g` -- unless
※ it says otherwise, and the specification says in so many words that the
※ convention need not match the system's and may differ between the functions
※ of one compilation.  `@[cdecl]` is how a function says otherwise: it follows
※ the one this system uses, and keeps its plain name, because the point of
※ asking for it is to be called by something that has never heard of this
※ language.
※
※ The two are called through their own conventions from one place here, which
※ is the thing that has to work: which register an argument goes in is what the
※ function being called says, and not what the caller follows.

@[cdecl]
fn system_way(a: u6, b: u6) → u6:
    a | b

fn our_way(a: u6, b: u6) → u6:
    a | b

@[cdecl]
fn asks_ours(a: u6, b: u6) → u6:
    our_way(a, b)

fn asks_theirs(a: u6, b: u6) → u6:
    system_way(a, b)

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    asks_ours(32u6, 8u6) | asks_theirs(2u6, 0u6)
